WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
[How To Prepare For Your Google Interview
Coding]

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.025
You've done it.

00:00:02.025 --> 00:00:04.421
You've landed an on-site
interview with Google.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:05.800
Congratulations!

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.325
My fellow Googlers
and I would like to share

00:00:08.325 --> 00:00:10.510
our tips and advice on how to prepare.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:14.500
In this video, we'll cover
communication and collaboration,

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.525
creating algorithms
and using data structures,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.775
writing clean and structured code,
testing and error checking,

00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:24.149
and overall best practices.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.425
Communication and collaboration
are incredibly important

00:00:27.425 --> 00:00:29.195
in the work we do here at Google.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.075
And that's because it's key
to how we approach developing

00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:34.034
and building our products.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:37.370
In your interview, it's important
to demonstrate those qualities.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.725
Understanding a problem,
designing a solution,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.825
and writing code are all
valuable parts of the interview process.

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:47.116
We want you to think out loud.

00:00:47.116 --> 00:00:49.466
We don't just want to see the final code.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.550
We want to understand
how you arrived at it.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.700
Google is a collaborative workplace

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.766
where projects are planned
and executed by teams.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.600
Those teams have a mission
to work together to solve big,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.775
often ambiguous problems,
not just write code.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.400
And those problems can
have multiple valid solutions.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.625
So, tell us your plan
on how to solve these problems

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.575
and be sure to ask questions.

00:01:13.575 --> 00:01:17.243
And remember, we're not
looking for one specific answer.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.075
Some of the problems you'll
encounter during your interview

00:01:20.075 --> 00:01:22.582
will be deliberately underspecified.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:24.800
We leave those questions open ended

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.700
because real engineering problems
require you to dig deeper.

00:01:29.435 --> 00:01:31.170
[2. Algorithms and Data Structures]

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.350
At Google, we deal with planet-scale data
and compute systems every day.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.400
Simply identifying a solution
isn't always enough.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.025
The code we write needs
to be fast enough to respond to users

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.300
and make efficient use
of our resources.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.975
You should have a strong understanding
of commonly used algorithms

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.850
and data structures
to do just that.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.125
When you're presented with a problem,
it's probably a good idea

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.725
to outline the algorithm
and discuss it with your interviewer

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.150
before you jump into the coding.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.525
It will give you an opportunity
to refine and improve your solution

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.875
collaboratively with your interviewer.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:11.775
Understanding the computational complexity
of your solution is also important.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.925
Remember, you don't have to come up
with the optimal solution up front.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.450
Iterating to improve your solution
can be a valuable part of the process.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.200
You're also going to want to use

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.925
appropriate algorithms
and data structures.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.350
You should have a strong
fundamental understanding

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.625
of commonly used data structures like

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:33.575
arrays, linked lists, stacks, and queues.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:39.675
You'll want to know sets, maps,
trees, heaps, and graphs as well.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.725
It's also essential to know
which algorithms tend to go along

00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:44.475
with each data structure.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.175
Algorithms that are used to
solve Google problems typically include:

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.100
sorting, searching, and binary search.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.775
You should also be familiar
with divide-and-conquer,

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.575
dynamic programming and memorization,

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.125
greedy algorithms, and recursion.

00:02:59.125 --> 00:03:01.120
And be sure to brush up on algorithms

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.700
linked to specific data structures
like graph traversal.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.925
You won't be expected to answer
questions requiring some obscure

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.995
and highly domain-specific algorithm.

00:03:09.995 --> 00:03:12.690
When you're working out
the requirements of an algorithm,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.659
this could also be the perfect time
to think of good test inputs.

00:03:16.659 --> 00:03:17.820
[3. Coding]

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.575
At Google, developers may
work across different platforms,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.275
but the foundational knowledge required
to perform their job function is the same.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.425
Candidates will be expected
to write idiomatic code

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.000
in a programming language
and style they are comfortable with.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.775
Common languages candidates
choose to write code in include

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:42.030
C/C++, Java, Python, JavaScript, or Go.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.150
Choose the language
you are comfortable with.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.900
We are evaluating you
on conceptual understanding.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.825
And we know that if you're fluent
in one programming language,

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:52.225
you could learn another.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.650
We also are not trying to confuse you
with language trivia.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:04:00.700
So don't worry too much about
memorization, syntax or punctuation.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.050
We want the substance
of your code to shine through.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.130
[4. Testing and Error Checking]

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.200
Google's products
and services are important

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.000
for billions of people around the world.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.550
When we make changes,
we need to be confident

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.200
that we're not breaking
important existing features.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.450
After you're finished coding
in your interview,

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.650
you'll want to run through at least
one or two test cases to check your work.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:23.975
Good test inputs should exercise
the boundary conditions

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.550
in your inputs and your algorithm.

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.350
Use simple and compact
sample inputs first

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.350
to ensure your code is correct.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.375
And lastly, you may also be asked
to discuss malformed inputs

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.945
and how your code handles them.

00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:37.970
[5. Best Practices]

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:41.425
Now that you have the focus areas,
here are some overall best practices

00:04:41.425 --> 00:04:43.340
to keep in mind
for your actual interview day.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.290
[Explain]

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.225
We want to understand
how you think,

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.675
so it's important to explain your
thought process during the interview.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.375
We're not only evaluating
your technical ability

00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:54.130
but also how you solve problems.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.130
[Clarify]

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.400
Many questions will be
deliberately open-ended

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.675
to give us an idea
of how you think.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.470
We'd encourage you
to ask for clarification.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.300
[Improve]

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:05.875
And we all know that our first
solution may not be the best one.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.200
So, once you've come up
with an answer to a question,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.250
think about ways to improve upon it.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.525
Let your interviewer know
what you're thinking.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.250
And lastly, practice
on paper or a whiteboard.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.900
You may have the option to use a computer
and/or whiteboard during your interviews,

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.775
so you'll want to make sure
you practice for both possible options

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.550
and that your code
is easily readable if written.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.825
And those are our tips
to help you prepare

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.925
for a coding interview at Google!

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.625
If you have any questions
about your upcoming interview,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.750
you can always reach out
to your recruiter.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.050
We're here to help.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.100
And we look forward to seeing you at
one of our offices around the world soon.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.019
